# probe_wifi_assoc overrides on top of probes/common/sdkconfig.defaults.
#
# Same three properties as `probe_wifi_conn`, for the same reasons, and one difference.
#
# 1. **The partition table.** `partitions.csv` beside this file is the Passport's own layout, so
#    the identity guard accepts a flash of this image.
#
# 2. **Nothing is written to the device's NVS.** The Wi-Fi driver's NVS store and the PHY
#    calibration store are both off, the probe calls `esp_wifi_set_storage(WIFI_STORAGE_RAM)`,
#    and it never calls `nvs_flash_erase`. So the station configuration this probe is given --
#    which, unlike `probe_wifi_conn`'s, may include a credential at build time -- **is never
#    written to the device's flash outside the app image itself**, and the restore that follows
#    the capture overwrites that app. This is the property that makes a credentialled run
#    containable at all, and it is why the option is here and not left to a person to remember.
#
# 3. **The difference from `probe_wifi_conn`:** this probe is allowed to associate, so it needs
#    the IP stack's DHCP client to run. Nothing is enabled for it beyond ESP-IDF's defaults; the
#    line is here to say the absence is deliberate and checked, not overlooked.
#
# **No SSID and no password live here or anywhere else in the repository.** Both are empty
# Kconfig strings in main/Kconfig.projbuild, and an empty SSID makes the probe print a `NOTE|` and
# stop before it touches the radio. A capture against a real network supplies them through a
# third defaults file kept outside the checkout; see the Kconfig help (docs/secrets.md).
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_CUSTOM=y
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_CUSTOM_FILENAME="partitions.csv"
CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_NVS_ENABLED=n
CONFIG_ESP_PHY_CALIBRATION_AND_DATA_STORAGE=n
